  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="section" id="faq" style="padding-top: 40px">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <span class="kicker">Questions</span>
        <h2>Good to know.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq" data-reveal>
        <details>
          <summary>Why does LanDeck ask for Local Network access?</summary>
          <div>
            <p>
              macOS asks before any app talks to the devices on your network. Without it, LanDeck can’t see them. If you
              said Don’t Allow, LanDeck shows how to turn it on in System Settings › Privacy &amp; Security › Local Network.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why does it ask for my location?</summary>
          <div>
            <p>
              Only to show Wi‑Fi network names: yours, and in Nearby Wi‑Fi the networks around you. macOS shares them only
              with apps that have Location access. LanDeck never uses or stores your location, and works without it; the
              network then goes by its address instead.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it safe to scan my network?</summary>
          <div>
            <p>
              Yes. LanDeck only looks: it sends the same small, harmless requests your Mac makes every day (a ping, a
              connection that’s closed at once, a question to a printer). It never signs in to a device, never changes its
              settings, and never opens anything on your router. The only commands it sends are the ones you give: wake a
              device, or play and pause a TV.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will it find every device?</summary>
          <div>
            <p>
              Almost always. A device that’s asleep or switched off can’t answer; LanDeck then shows it as offline with when it
              was last seen. Some guest networks keep devices from seeing each other, and nothing on the Mac can get past that.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does LanDeck work on networks other than mine?</summary>
          <div>
            <p>
              It scans whatever network your Mac is on. Only scan networks you own or look after, or where you have permission.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note" style="text-align: center; margin-top: 28px">
        More answers on the <a href={url('support/')}>support page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Final call -->
